{"test_id": "B23-001", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "How do CCoP 2.0 and MAS TRM requirements align for incident reporting?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "MAS 1-hour deadline is stricter than CCoP 2-hour requirement", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}, {"fact": "Bank must comply with stricter deadline (1 hour) for dual compliance", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}, {"fact": "Incident classification determines which timeline applies", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not set MAS, PDPC or other external reporting timelines in the Code itself. The grounded position is that the CIIO must maintain the incident-reporting structure required by the CCoP for the designated CII and separately comply with each external reporting regime that applies to the scenario. Where timelines differ, the CIIO should design its internal process to meet the applicable obligations in parallel, document the mapping between regimes, and escalate any irreconcilable conflict under its cybersecurity risk-management framework and, where necessary, through the waiver or guidance process. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6, §3.2.1, §7.1.1(b); Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: conflicting_timelines", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1", "7.1.1(b)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "clause_reference_original": ["11.1"], "clause_reference_corrected_by": "agent-team-2026-04-26", "clause_reference_corrected_verdict": "GT_INCORRECT — REPLACE", "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B23-002", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "What are the differences between CCoP risk assessment and MAS TRM risk management requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "MAS TRM requires continuous risk monitoring vs CCoP annual assessment", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}, {"fact": "Risk registers must satisfy both regulator requirements", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}, {"fact": "MAS TRM includes operational risks beyond cybersecurity scope", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 provides the cybersecurity baseline for designated CII only. It does not restate or harmonise the detailed obligations in MAS TRM, PDPA, IM8 or other external regimes inside the CCoP corpus. The grounded answer is therefore to comply with the applicable CCoP requirements for the designated CII, separately comply with the external regime's own requirements, map overlapping evidence where useful, and avoid assuming equivalence or precedence unless the external regime expressly says so. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6, §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: overlapping_with_differences", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7); external regime-specific requirements remain outside the audited corpus"]}}
{"test_id": "B23-003", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "Can a single audit satisfy both CSA and MAS requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Joint supervision exists for some banking areas", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}, {"fact": "CSA and MAS coordinate on some audit activities", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}, {"fact": "Separate cybersecurity documentation usually required for each regulator", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not provide an automatic single-audit or mutual-recognition mechanism across regulators. A CIIO may reuse overlapping evidence where multiple regimes examine similar controls, but compliance with the CCoP and compliance with an external regime remain separate unless that external regime expressly recognises the same audit output. The safe answer is therefore to map common evidence once, then satisfy each regulator's own audit or attestation requirement separately. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §15.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: partial_harmonization", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7); external regime-specific requirements remain outside the audited corpus"]}}
{"test_id": "B23-004", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "Which prevails when CCoP and MAS requirements conflict?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CCoP prevails for CII cybersecurity matters", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}, {"fact": "MAS TRM still applies to banking operations regardless of CCoP", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}, {"fact": "Conflicts must be documented and raised with regulators", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: banking"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 applies to the designated CII under the Cybersecurity Act, while other sectoral or government requirements continue to apply under their own legal or policy framework. The CCoP corpus does not contain a universal precedence rule that automatically displaces other regulators. The grounded answer is that the CIIO must comply with the applicable CCoP requirements for the designated CII, separately comply with the other governing framework, and manage any conflict through its cybersecurity risk-management process and the waiver or guidance mechanisms where needed. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6, §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: ccop_prevails_for_cii", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7); external regime-specific requirements remain outside the audited corpus"]}}
{"test_id": "B23-005", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "How do CCoP 2.0 and IM8 government security requirements align?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "IM8 provides government baseline security controls", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}, {"fact": "CCoP 2.0 provides CII-specific requirements on top of IM8", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}, {"fact": "GovTech provides guidance on IM8 and CCoP integration", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 provides the cybersecurity baseline for designated CII only. It does not restate or harmonise the detailed obligations in MAS TRM, PDPA, IM8 or other external regimes inside the CCoP corpus. The grounded answer is therefore to comply with the applicable CCoP requirements for the designated CII, separately comply with the external regime's own requirements, map overlapping evidence where useful, and avoid assuming equivalence or precedence unless the external regime expressly says so. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6, §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: complementary_frameworks", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7); external regime-specific requirements remain outside the audited corpus"]}}
{"test_id": "B23-006", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "Who handles CII compliance for government agencies - CSA or GovTech?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CSA regulates CII compliance for all sectors including government", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}, {"fact": "GovTech provides technical guidance for government CII", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}, {"fact": "Government CII operators are subject to CSA enforcement", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 applies to the designated CII under the Cybersecurity Act, while other sectoral or government requirements continue to apply under their own legal or policy framework. The CCoP corpus does not contain a universal precedence rule that automatically displaces other regulators. The grounded answer is that the CIIO must comply with the applicable CCoP requirements for the designated CII, separately comply with the other governing framework, and manage any conflict through its cybersecurity risk-management process and the waiver or guidance mechanisms where needed. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6, §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: csa_regulator_govtech_advisor", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7); external regime-specific requirements remain outside the audited corpus"]}}
{"test_id": "B23-007", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "How do government-specific security classifications align with CCoP requirements?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Government security classifications must be mapped to CCoP protection requirements", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}, {"fact": "Higher government classifications require stricter controls than CCoP baseline", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}, {"fact": "CII designation may cover different scope than government classification", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not define government security-classification levels or a formal alignment model with external government classification schemes. A CIIO must comply with the applicable CCoP requirements for the designated CII and separately comply with any government classification rules that apply under their own governing framework. If an external requirement conflicts with a CCoP requirement, the CIIO should assess the issue under its cybersecurity risk-management framework and, where necessary, seek CSA guidance or a waiver under CCoP §1.6 and section 11(7) of the Cybersecurity Act. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6, §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: mapping_required", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7); external regime-specific requirements remain outside the audited corpus"]}}
