{"test_id": "B23-013", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "What happens when CSA requires notification but PDPC does not?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "CII critical incident classification determines CSA obligation", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: all"}, {"fact": "Personal data breach threshold determines PDPC obligation", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: all"}, {"fact": "Overlapping area triggers both notifications", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: all"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not set MAS, PDPC or other external reporting timelines in the Code itself. The grounded position is that the CIIO must maintain the incident-reporting structure required by the CCoP for the designated CII and separately comply with each external reporting regime that applies to the scenario. Where timelines differ, the CIIO should design its internal process to meet the applicable obligations in parallel, document the mapping between regimes, and escalate any irreconcilable conflict under its cybersecurity risk-management framework and, where necessary, through the waiver or guidance process. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6, §3.2.1, §7.1.1(b); Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: ccop_prevails_not_pdpa", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1", "7.1.1(b)"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7)"]}}
{"test_id": "B23-014", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "Can mutually recognized audits satisfy multiple regulators?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Coordinated supervision exists between MAS and CSA for some areas", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government, banking"}, {"fact": "Recognition depends on audit scope and regulator agreement", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government, banking"}, {"fact": "Separate documentation usually required for each regulator", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: government, banking"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 does not provide an automatic single-audit or mutual-recognition mechanism across regulators. A CIIO may reuse overlapping evidence where multiple regimes examine similar controls, but compliance with the CCoP and compliance with an external regime remain separate unless that external regime expressly recognises the same audit output. The safe answer is therefore to map common evidence once, then satisfy each regulator's own audit or attestation requirement separately. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §15.", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: partial_mutual_recognition", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "low", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7); external regime-specific requirements remain outside the audited corpus"]}}
{"test_id": "B23-015", "version": "2.0", "benchmark_id": "B23", "input": {"question": "How do CCoP requirements align with sector-specific regulators (e.g., EMA for energy)?", "scenario_sector": "energy", "scenario_role": "risk_manager"}, "ground_truth": {"key_facts": [{"fact": "Sector-specific regulator requirements add to CCoP baseline", "tier": "critical", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: energy"}, {"fact": "CII designation scope determines which requirements apply", "tier": "important", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: energy"}, {"fact": "Energy sector has additional operational safety regulators", "tier": "supporting", "source": "Multi-regulatory framework: energy"}], "expected_response": "CCoP 2.0 provides the cybersecurity baseline for designated CII only. It does not restate or harmonise the detailed obligations in MAS TRM, PDPA, IM8 or other external regimes inside the CCoP corpus. The grounded answer is therefore to comply with the applicable CCoP requirements for the designated CII, separately comply with the external regime's own requirements, map overlapping evidence where useful, and avoid assuming equivalence or precedence unless the external regime expressly says so. Reference: CCoP 2.0 §1.6, §3.2.1; Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7).", "reasoning_chain": ["Identify regulatory frameworks involved", "Analyze alignment type: sector_specific_on_ccop_base", "Map overlapping and distinct requirements"]}, "fail_conditions": {"forbidden_claims": ["Stating one regulator completely replaces another", "Assuming alignment without checking for conflicts", "Downplaying regulator-specific requirements", "Clear articulation of regulatory alignment or conflict", "Coordination strategy for overlapping requirements", "Reference to specific regulatory frameworks"], "hallucination_patterns": ["Inventing specific regulatory provisions", "Creating non-existent regulatory overlaps"]}, "metadata": {"section": "N/A (multi-regulator coordination is not addressed in CCoP 2.0; remove or set to null)", "clause_reference": ["1.6.1", "1.6.2", "1.6.3", "3.2.1"], "domain": "IT", "difficulty": "medium", "scenario_type": "regulatory_coordination", "related_sections": [], "test_category": "edge_case", "created_date": "2026-04-01", "reviewer": null, "support_citations": ["Cybersecurity Act 2018 §11(7); external regime-specific requirements remain outside the audited corpus"]}}
